j=A.bD(A.D(["Python",'# Define the Node class\nclass Node:\n    def __init__(self, data):\n        self.data = data  # The value\n        self.next = None  # Pointer to the next node\n\n# Create nodes and link them\nhead = Node(10)\nhead.next = Node(20)\n\nprint(f"Head: {head.data}, Next: {head.next.data}")\n',"Java","// Define the Node class\nclass Node {\n    int data;\n    Node next;\n\n    Node(int data) {\n        this.data = data;\n        this.next = null;\n    }\n}\n\n// Usage\nNode head = new Node(10);\nhead.next = new Node(20);\n","C++","#include <iostream>\nusing namespace std;\n\n// Define the Node struct\nstruct Node {\n    int data;\n    Node* next;\n    \n    Node(int val) {\n        data = val;\n        next = nullptr;\n    }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Node* head = new Node(10);\n    head->next = new Node(20);\n    return 0;\n}\n","JavaScript","// Define the Node class\nclass Node {\n    constructor(data) {\n        this.data = data;\n        this.next = null;\n    }\n}\n\n// Create and link\nlet head = new Node(10);\nhead.next = new Node(20);\n"],g,g),h,i,"Unlike arrays, Linked Lists do not store data in a continuous block of memory. Instead, data is stored in individual 'Nodes' that use pointers to link to the next node in the chain. This makes inserting and deleting data incredibly fast, but comes at the cost of losing direct index access.","linkedlist-intro",m,"Linked List Introduction",A.a(["Losing the 'Head' pointer, which completely deletes the entire list in memory!","Null Pointer Exceptions (trying to access node.next when node is null)","Forgetting to update the 'next' pointer when inserting, breaking the chain."],k),j,A.a([A.M(1,"Unlike arrays, Linked Lists do not require contiguous memory. Nodes can be allocated anywhere in RAM, and they find each other using references (pointers).",A.a(["In a single, continuous, contiguous block of memory.","In independent nodes scattered across memory, connected by pointers.","In a mathematical grid system.","Inside the CPU cache exclusively."],k),"How does a Linked List store its data in computer memory?"),A.M(2,"In an array, insertion requires an O(n) shift. In a linked list, if you know where you want to insert, you just update two pointers in O(1) time. No shifting required!",A.a(["You can instantly access any element by its index.","It requires less overall memory than an array.","Inserting or deleting a node does not require shifting other elements.","It is automatically sorted by the CPU."],k),"What is the primary advantage of a Linked List over a standard Array?"),A.M(1,"Because you cannot jump directly to an index, you must start at the Head node and traverse the 'next' pointers 500 times. This makes access O(n).",A.a(["O(1)","O(n)","O(log n)","O(n\xb2)"],k),"What is the time complexity to access the 500th element in a Linked List?"),A.M(3,"The Head is your only entry point to the scavenger hunt. If you lose the Head, you have no way to find the rest of the nodes in memory!",A.a(["The list automatically reverses itself.","The list becomes a Circular Linked List.","The computer creates a new Head automatically.","You lose access to the entire list, causing a memory leak (in languages like C++)."],k),"What happens if the 'Head' pointer of a Linked List is accidentally reassigned to null?")],f),"assets/mds/dsa/linked_list/linkedlist_intro.md",B.a0X)
