i=A.bD(A.D(["Python","def search(head, target):\n    current = head\n    index = 0\n    \n    # Traverse the list until we hit the end (None)\n    while current is not None:\n        if current.value == target:\n            return index # Target found!\n            \n        current = current.next\n        index += 1\n        \n    return -1 # Target not found\n","Java","int search(Node head, int target) {\n    Node current = head;\n    int index = 0;\n    \n    // Traverse the list until we hit the end (null)\n    while (current != null) {\n        if (current.value == target) {\n            return index; // Target found!\n        }\n        current = current.next;\n        index++;\n    }\n    \n    return -1; // Target not found\n}\n","C++","int search(Node* head, int target) {\n    Node* current = head;\n    int index = 0;\n    \n    // Traverse the list until we hit the end (nullptr)\n    while (current != nullptr) {\n        if (current->value == target) {\n            return index; // Target found!\n        }\n        current = current->next;\n        index++;\n    }\n    \n    return -1; // Target not found\n}\n","JavaScript","function search(head, target) {\n    let current = head;\n    let index = 0;\n    \n    // Traverse the list until we hit the end (null)\n    while (current !== null) {\n        if (current.value === target) {\n            return index; // Target found!\n        }\n        current = current.next;\n        index++;\n    }\n    \n    return -1; // Target not found\n}\n"],g,g),q,h,"Master the art of node traversal! Because Linked Lists are scattered throughout memory, you cannot instantly jump to a specific index like you can with an Array. Learn how to safely deploy a temporary pointer to walk through a Linked List step-by-step to find your target data in O(n) time.","linkedlist-search",m,"Linked List Searching",A.a(["Losing the Head: If you use the actual 'head' pointer to traverse the list (e.g., 'head = head.next') instead of a temporary 'current' pointer, you permanently lose the start of your list!","Null Reference Errors: Attempting to check 'current.next.value' without first ensuring 'current.next' is not null will instantly crash your program.","Assuming Random Access: Beginners often try to run a for-loop and access 'list[i]', forgetting that Linked Lists do not have continuous memory indexes."],k),i,A.a([A.M(1,"Binary Search relies on calculating a midpoint index and instantly jumping to it. Since Linked Lists require you to physically walk through every single node to reach the middle, the divide-and-conquer speed advantage is completely destroyed.",A.a(["Because Linked Lists can only hold strings, not integers.","Because Linked Lists lack random access (indexes), making it impossible to instantly jump to the middle element in O(1) time.","Because the memory addresses in a Linked List are always sorted backwards.","Because Binary Search requires O(n) auxiliary memory, which Linked Lists do not support."],k),"Why is Binary Search generally impossible (or highly inefficient) on a standard Singly Linked List?"),A.M(2,"The 'head' variable is your only anchor to the start of the list. If you reassign it to point to the second or third node during your search, you drop the map to the beginning, effectively destroying your access to the rest of the dataset.",A.a(["Using the head pointer directly causes a memory leak.","The compiler will throw a syntax error if you modify the head pointer.","If you move the head pointer forward during the search, you lose the only reference to the beginning of your list, and the preceding nodes will be deleted by the Garbage Collector.","The head node does not actually contain a value, so it cannot be compared."],k),"When searching a Linked List, why must we assign 'Node current = head;' instead of just using the 'head' pointer directly to traverse?"),A.M(2,"If the element doesn't exist, you are forced to check every single node one by one until you hit the 'null' tail. This requires a full traversal, resulting in a worst-case O(n) linear time complexity.",A.a(["O(1)","O(log n)","O(n)","O(n\xb2)"],k),"What is the Time Complexity of searching for an element in a Linked List if the element does NOT exist?"),A.M(3,"If the target is the very first node, the algorithm finds it on the very first comparison and instantly returns. This is the Best-Case scenario, executing in instant O(1) constant time regardless of how massive the list is.",A.a(["O(n)","O(log n)","O(n\xb2)","O(1)"],k),"If the target element is located at the very beginning of the Linked List (the Head node), what is the search time complexity?")],f),"assets/mds/dsa/linked_list/linkedlistsearch.md",B.a0V)
